#!/bin/sh
# wifihaven-disable — turn OFF all WifiHaven enforcement on THIS router (#2381).
#
# The escape hatch. Sets the local UCI flag wifihaven.settings.enforcement_disabled=1
# and commits it. On its next apply cycle (within ~1s) the agent tears down ALL
# blocking — every nftables forward-drop (paused/scheduled/time-limit whole-MAC
# blocks, per-site extraBlocked, category blocklists, blockIpOnly) AND the
# block-page HTTP redirect — so all forwarded traffic passes and every device
# has normal internet.
#
# This works even when the API server is DOWN: the flag lives on the router and
# is read locally, independent of the policy snapshot. Use it for a server
# outage, a bad policy, or "the internet is broken and I need it back right now."
#
# It does NOT uninstall anything and does NOT lose your settings — it is a
# reversible pause. Restore normal blocking any time with:  wifihaven-enable
#
# The server-driven per-household equivalent is the dashboard toggle (#2382),
# which is easier but needs the API to be up. This is the offline fallback.
set -e

uci set wifihaven.settings=settings 2>/dev/null || true
uci set wifihaven.settings.enforcement_disabled='1'
uci commit wifihaven

cat <<'EOF'
WifiHaven enforcement DISABLED (escape hatch on).

All blocking on this router is being torn down; every device will have normal,
unfiltered internet within a few seconds. This persists across reboots and works
even if the WifiHaven server is unreachable.

Re-enable normal blocking with:   wifihaven-enable

Watch it take effect:             logread -f | grep wifihaven
EOF
